---
import extension from "../../../../chrome-extension/package.json";
import Docs from "../layouts/Docs.astro";
---

<Docs
  title="The local proxy"
  description="For services on your computer or network that do not allow calls from PeerShare."
>
  <h1>The local proxy</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A small program on your computer, for services that do not allow the PeerShare page to call
    them. Remote https sites that allow PeerShare do not need it.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it is needed</h2>
  <p>
    Browsers let a page read another site only when that site allows it with CORS headers. A dev
    server on <code>localhost:5173</code>, a router's admin page or a NAS usually doesn't. The
    proxy runs next to them, on your computer, and fetches them for the PeerShare page.
  </p>

  <h2>Start it</h2>
  <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://peershare.sandclaw.ai/proxy/peershare-proxy.sh | sh</code></pre>
  <p>
    The launcher downloads the proxy (one file) into <code>~/.cache/peershare/</code> and runs it
    with Deno, Bun or Node, whichever it finds first. With none of them, it downloads Deno once
    into the same folder. Nothing is installed system-wide. It runs on macOS and Linux.
  </p>
  <p>
    The proxy prints a link. Open it and the PeerShare page is set up to use the proxy. You can
    also type the address and key into the <strong>Local proxy</strong> card yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://peershare.sandclaw.ai/proxy/peershare-proxy.sh | sh -s -- --port 9091 --key s3cret</code></pre>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>--port</code></td><td><code>9090</code></td><td>The port on <code>127.0.0.1</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--key</code></td><td>none</td><td>When set, the page must send it</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--origin</code></td>
        <td>PeerShare, and pages on this computer</td>
        <td>Which pages may use it; replaces the list</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--no-cookie-jars</code></td>
        <td>jars on</td>
        <td>Turns off the separate cookie jar for each guest</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What it will and won't do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      It listens on <code>127.0.0.1</code> only: nothing else on your network can talk to it.
    </li>
    <li>
      It reaches <code>localhost</code>, private addresses (<code>192.168.…</code>,
      <code>10.…</code>), Tailscale addresses (<code>100.x</code>), and <code>.local</code> names.
      A public address is refused.
    </li>
    <li>
      It answers the PeerShare page and pages served from your computer; other websites you visit
      get <code>403</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      It keeps <strong>each guest's cookies apart</strong>, so a service that signs people in with
      cookies works for every guest separately.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="extension">Or: the browser extension</h2>
  <p>
    In Chrome (and Edge, Brave and other Chromium browsers) the
    <strong>SandClaw.ai PeerShare Proxy</strong> extension does the proxy's job, with nothing to
    start: install it, open PeerShare, and the <strong>Local proxy</strong> card says
    <em>Browser extension connected</em>. It follows the proxy's rules: it answers only the
    PeerShare page and pages on your computer, and reaches only <code>localhost</code> and private
    or Tailscale addresses.
  </p>
  <p>Until it is in the Chrome Web Store, install it by hand:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <a href="https://peershare.sandclaw.ai/extension/peershare-proxy-extension.zip" download
        >Download the extension {extension.version}</a
      >
      (<a href="https://peershare.sandclaw.ai/extension/CHANGELOG.txt">what's new</a>) and unzip
      it.
    </li>
    <li>Open <code>chrome://extensions</code> and switch on <strong>Developer mode</strong>.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Load unpacked</strong> and choose the unzipped folder.</li>
    <li>Reload PeerShare: the Local proxy card says <em>Browser extension connected</em>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Keep the unzipped folder: Chrome runs the extension from it. The version you have is on the
    Local proxy card (and on the extension's card in <code>chrome://extensions</code>); when the
    card says a newer one is available, or the link above names a newer one, download the new zip,
    unzip it over the old folder, and click the reload icon on the extension's card in
    <code>chrome://extensions</code>.
  </p>
  <p>What the extension does differently from the proxy:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No cookie jars.</strong> An extension cannot see the cookies a service sets, so
      services that sign people in with cookies do not keep guests signed in.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Redirects are followed</strong> for the guest, so the address a guest sees may not
      change when the service redirects.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No live connections</strong> (WebSockets): live parts of a page stay still unless
      the proxy runs too.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>.local</code> names are refused</strong>: the extension cannot look up where
      they point. Use the device's address, or the proxy.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Browsers</h2>
  <p>
    Chrome and Edge ask once whether the page may reach devices on your local network: choose
    <strong>Allow</strong>. Firefox works too. Safari blocks a secure page's calls to
    <code>127.0.0.1</code>, so the proxy cannot be used there. Remote https services that allow
    PeerShare still work in Safari.
  </p>
</Docs>
